    "sourceNote": "Rijksmuseum collections and Early Netherlandish Paintings catalogue; Antheun Janse's scholarship on Jacoba van Beieren; Treaty of Delft and Treaty of The Hague chronologies.",
    "notes": "Jacqueline succeeded her father in 1417 and fought a prolonged succession struggle involving her uncle John of Bavaria and Philip the Good. Philip gained effective control after the 1428 settlement and the crisis of 1432; Jacqueline formally relinquished Hainaut, Holland and Zeeland in 1433. The project anchor 'Philip the Good acquired Hainaut in 1432' is useful for de facto political control but not for the formal title chronology. The Treaty of Delft in 1428 left Jacqueline nominal countess while Philip governed and became heir; events in 1432 gave Philip effective control, while the Treaty of The Hague on 12 April 1433 formalized Jacqueline's renunciation. Her countess titles should therefore normally end in 1433, not 1432.",
